{"case_id": "m03", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "ok", "grade_level": 8, "tags": ["Whole numbers"], "output": "**Solution:**\n\n1.  **Calculate the total number of parts in the ratio:**\n    $$2 + 3 + 4 = 9 \\text{ parts}$$\n\n2.  **Calculate the value of one part:**\n    $$\\text{R}360 \\div 9 = \\text{R}40$$\n\n3.  **Calculate the value of the largest share (4 parts):**\n    $$4 \\times \\text{R}40 = \\text{R}160$$\n\n**Final Answer:**\nThe person with the largest share gets **R160**.", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 1.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 8.566, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 498, "output_tokens": 1437, "reasoning_tokens": 1303, "cost_usd": 0.001512, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["stop"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:19"}
{"case_id": "m02", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "ok", "grade_level": 8, "tags": ["Whole numbers"], "output": "**Question:** Find the LCM of 12 and 18.\n\n**Solution:**\n\n**Step 1:** Find the prime factorisation of each number.\n$$12 = 2 \\times 6 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 = 2^2 \\times 3$$\n$$18 = 2 \\times 9 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 = 2 \\times 3^2$$\n\n**Step 2:** Identify the highest power of each prime factor present.\n- Highest power of 2: $2^2$\n- Highest power of 3: $3^2$\n\n**Step 3:** Multiply the highest powers of all prime factors to find the LCM.\n$$\\text{LCM} = 2^2 \\times 3^2$$\n$$\\text{LCM} = 4 \\times 9$$\n$$\\text{LCM} = 36$$\n\n**Answer:**\n$$36$$", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 1.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 9.806, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 407, "output_tokens": 1586, "reasoning_tokens": 1364, "cost_usd": 0.001647, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["stop"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:20"}
{"case_id": "m04", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "truncated", "grade_level": 8, "tags": ["Whole numbers"], "output": "", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 0.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 11.155, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 493, "output_tokens": 2000, "reasoning_tokens": 1998, "cost_usd": 0.002074, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["length"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:21"}
{"case_id": "m01", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "ok", "grade_level": 8, "tags": ["Whole numbers"], "output": "**QUESTION: Find the HCF of 84 and 126.**\n\n**Solution:**\n\n1.  **Prime factorisation of 84:**\n    $$84 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 3 \\times 7$$\n    $$84 = 2^2 \\times 3 \\times 7$$\n\n2.  **Prime factorisation of 126:**\n    $$126 = 2 \\times 3 \\times 3 \\times 7$$\n    $$126 = 2 \\times 3^2 \\times 7$$\n\n3.  **Identify common prime factors:**\n    The prime factors that appear in both lists are:\n    *   $$2$$\n    *   $$3$$\n    *   $$7$$\n\n4.  **Calculate the HCF:**\n    $$\\text{HCF} = 2 \\times 3 \\times 7$$\n    $$\\text{HCF} = 6 \\times 7$$\n    $$\\text{HCF} = 42$$\n\n**Answer:** 42", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 1.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 11.265, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 431, "output_tokens": 1869, "reasoning_tokens": 1623, "cost_usd": 0.001934, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["stop"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:21"}
{"case_id": "m41", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "ok", "grade_level": 12, "tags": ["Patterns, sequences and series", "grade12"], "output": "**Solution:**\n\nGeneral term of an arithmetic sequence:\n$$T_n = a + (n - 1)d$$\n\nGiven:\n$a = 7$\n$d = 4$\n$n = 25$\n\nSubstitute the values into the formula:\n$$T_{25} = 7 + (25 - 1)4$$\n$$T_{25} = 7 + (24)4$$\n$$T_{25} = 7 + 96$$\n$$T_{25} = 103$$\n\n**Answer:**\n$$T_{25} = 103$$", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 1.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 6.717, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 419, "output_tokens": 1149, "reasoning_tokens": 1008, "cost_usd": 0.001212, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["stop"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:27"}
{"case_id": "m42", "rep": 0, "suite": "maths", "variant": "qwen__qwen3.5-35b-a3b@low", "model": "qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b", "effort": "low", "group": "open", "status": "ok", "grade_level": 12, "tags": ["Patterns, sequences and series", "grade12"], "output": "The sequence $3 + 7 + 11 + \\dots$ is an arithmetic progression (AP).\n\n**Given:**\nFirst term, $a = 3$\nCommon difference, $d = 7 - 3 = 4$\nNumber of terms, $n = 20$\n\n**Formula:**\n$$S_n = \\frac{n}{2}[2a + (n-1)d]$$\n\n**Calculation:**\n$$S_{20} = \\frac{20}{2}[2(3) + (20-1)4]$$\n$$S_{20} = 10[6 + (19)4]$$\n$$S_{20} = 10[6 + 76]$$\n$$S_{20} = 10[82]$$\n$$S_{20} = 820$$\n\n**Final Answer:**\nThe sum of the first 20 terms is **820**.", "pred": null, "grade": {"correct": 1.0}, "judge": null, "latency_s": 7.926, "calls": 1, "input_tokens": 427, "output_tokens": 1431, "reasoning_tokens": 1214, "cost_usd": 0.001495, "served_models": ["qwen/qwen3.5-35b-a3b"], "hosts": ["Venice"], "finish_reasons": ["stop"], "judge_cost_usd": 0.0, "ts": "2026-10-31T17:31:29"}
